  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will shift southward to the mid Atlantic region \nthis afternoon. The high will generally remain in place there \nthrough Tue. The result will be dry conditions except for a few \nlight showers possible in the Adirondacks associated with a \nnorthward advancing warm front Tue P.M. Otherwise, just some \nadded cloud cover will move across the rest of the area. With \nridging aloft and low level S/SW flow, temperatures will be \nabove normal for the next several days. 850 mb temperature \nanomalies are forecast to be +1 to +2 STDEV Mon through Fri \nacross the entire area (NAEFS). Surface and upper level ridging \nalong the east coast should hold through Wed. \n\nAfter nearly a 3-week stretch of dry weather, there is \nincreasing confidence in multiple chances for rain late this \nweek into the upcoming weekend. A moist SW flow aloft develops \nacross the region on Thu (PWAT anomalies increase to +1 to +3 \nSTDEV), ahead of a positively tilted trough moving east across \nthe Great Lakes. At the surface, an eastward advancing cold \nfront looks to bring a period of rain to the area, as most \nguidance is in agreement now. Whether rain persists on Fri is \nmore uncertain, as some sources of guidance slows the front's \nprogress or even stalls near or just east of our area. There is \nenough model agreement to at least mention likely PoPs. As an \nupper level trough settles in across the eastern Great Lakes and\nNortheast next weekend, there will be additional chances for \nrain either with the trough itself and/or with a potential \ncoastal storm developing near New England. Details will come \ninto more focus as the week progresses, but bottom line is much \nneeded rainfall is expected.",
